# L §4.5: a known window that holds the cut "states a windows clause or is HOLE-IMPRECISE ... This holds for
# everything the version or row brings: its rows, band tables and rules". v2, known within 2026-10-15..2026-11-01,
# brings f's rule more (n + n = 2, beating base = n = 1) and b's table (2 for [0, 10), replacing v1's 1). As known
# 2026-10-20: imprecise; windows earliest sees v2, latest doesn't. (Writer 6: was REFCORE-UNSPECIFIED §4.5 for rules
# and band tables.)
# (Writer 8: windows earliest and latest are gone; windows each splits the line at the day the version becomes
# known, the window's days up to the cut (seen) and after it (unseen), L §4.4 "That holds for every window ...: a
# version's known (§4.5)". The earliest line is the first part, the latest the second.)
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
input n: int
band b(x: int): int

def f: int
  rule base v v1 = n
  rule more beats base v v2 = n + n

version v1
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  n = 1 "s"
  b: [0, 10) = 1 "s"

version v2
  cite s
  governs always
  known window(2026-10-15, 2026-11-01)
  replace b: [0, 10) = 2 "s"

ask r1: f as known 2026-10-20
ask r2: f as known 2026-10-20 windows each
ask r4: f
ask b1: b(n) law 2026-06-30 as known 2026-10-20
ask b2: b(n) law 2026-06-30 as known 2026-10-20 windows each
ask b4: b(n) law 2026-06-30
=== expect
b1: ?"date within window(2026-10-15, 2026-11-01) not precise enough for the cut 2026-10-20" ...
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-10-20
b2 [known of v2 = 2026-10-15 .. 2026-10-20]: 2
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-10-20
b2 [known of v2 = 2026-10-21 .. 2026-11-01]: 1
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-10-20
b4: 2
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-12-31
r1: ?"date within window(2026-10-15, 2026-11-01) not precise enough for the cut 2026-10-20" ...
  cut 2026-10-20
r2 [known of v2 = 2026-10-15 .. 2026-10-20]: 2
  cut 2026-10-20
r2 [known of v2 = 2026-10-21 .. 2026-11-01]: 1
  cut 2026-10-20
r4: 2
  cut 2026-12-31
